🧠 Conceptual Theory
1. Objective

To create and manage student information (like ID, name, age, course) using a smart contract that:

Uses structs to define student data,

Uses arrays to store multiple students,

Implements fallback and receive functions for handling Ether transfers.

🧩 Concepts Used
a) Structure (struct)

A struct in Solidity is a user-defined data type that groups related data items.

📘 Example in your program:

struct Student {
    uint256 id;
    string name;
    uint8 age;
    string course;
}


This defines a template to store each student’s data in one variable.

b) Array

An array stores multiple student records.

Student[] private students;


This creates a dynamic array, meaning new students can be added at runtime using .push().

c) Fallback and Receive Functions

These special functions handle Ether transfers sent to the contract address.

receive() → triggered when contract receives plain Ether (no data).

fallback() → triggered when:

A function that doesn’t exist is called, or

Ether is sent with data but no matching function is found.

In your code:

receive() external payable {}
fallback() external payable {}


This ensures the contract safely accepts Ether (even unintentionally).

d) Event

An event is used to log activities on the blockchain (helpful for off-chain apps).

event StudentAdded(uint256 id, string name, uint8 age, string course);


This is emitted every time a new student is added — visible in the transaction logs.

e) Functions Explained
Function	Description
addStudent()	Adds a new student record to the array and emits an event.
getStudent()	Retrieves student data by index (used for display).
getTotalStudents()	Returns number of students stored.
receive() / fallback()	Handle incoming Ether transactions gracefully.
⚙️ Algorithm (Step-by-Step Flow)

Start

Define the Student structure with attributes (id, name, age, course).

Create a dynamic array students.

Define addStudent() to append new student data to the array.

Emit an event StudentAdded after adding each student.

Define getStudent() to retrieve data by index.

Implement getTotalStudents() to count stored records.

Add receive() and fallback() functions to handle Ether.

Deploy on Ethereum test network (e.g., Sepolia) using Remix IDE.

Observe gas usage for each transaction in the Remix terminal.

⛽ Gas and Transaction Fee Observation

When deployed and executed in Remix:

Operation	Description	Approx. Gas Used	Remarks
Deployment	Deploy contract to blockchain	~130,000 – 200,000 gas	Highest cost since it stores contract code
addStudent()	Adds new record	~45,000 – 70,000 gas	Depends on data length
getStudent() / getTotalStudents()	Read operations	~0 gas (view only)	Read-only, no state change
Fallback/Receive	Receive Ether	~20,000 gas	Minimal cost for transaction acceptance

💰 Transaction Fee = Gas Used × Gas Price

Example:
If gas used = 60,000 and gas price = 20 gwei,
→ Fee = 60,000 × 20 × 10⁻⁹ ETH = 0.0012 ETH

